            <h2>2. “Artist Intelligence”: Generative AI Meets Manual Precision</h2>
            <p>
                This release’s buzz phrase is <strong>Artist Intelligence</strong> – the idea that AI features should boost creativity without taking control. In practice, CorelDRAW 2026 introduces a suite of generative-AI tools that integrate smoothly with traditional vector workflows.
            </p>

            <h3>AI Generate & Remix</h3>
            <p>
                A new “AI Generate” pane lets you type prompts to create original images. Results are commercially licensed and fully editable in CorelDRAW. You can fine-tune style (photorealistic, line art, 3D, pop art, etc.) and color palette, or use “Inspire me” to generate prompt suggestions. After generating an image, the <strong>Remix</strong> option can transform it (for example, changing an autumn scene to summer) within seconds. 
            </p>
            
            <blockquote class="p-4 my-6 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 bg-slate-50 italic text-slate-700 text-base sm:text-lg rounded-r-xl">
                "TechRadar found these generative results 'astonishing' – the software could convert an autumn sunset to a sunny summer scene with no loss in detail."
            </blockquote>

            <p>
                If an AI result isn’t perfect, you can still tweak it in CorelDRAW (vectorize with PowerTRACE, adjust shapes, etc.).
            </p>

            <h3>AI-assisted Image Cleanup</h3>
            <p>
                CorelDRAW’s background removal is now one-click AI. The tool instantly identifies the foreground (even tricky hair and fabric edges) and removes the rest. In tests, the results were impressive – comparable to top photo editors. Similarly, <strong>AI masking</strong> in PHOTO-PAINT auto-selects subjects and builds pixel-accurate masks that you can refine. These let you isolate and replace parts of bitmaps far faster than manual selection. Meanwhile, <strong>PowerTRACE</strong> uses AI upsampling to improve line art and logo tracing, converting low-res artwork into clean, editable vectors without a full redraw.
            </p>

            <div class="my-6 p-6 rounded-2xl bg-white border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
                <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-base mb-2">Workflow Impact: Solving Everyday File Headaches</h4>
                <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-3">
                    Together, these tools address common file headaches. Corel says 2026 addresses low-res, artifacts, unwanted backgrounds, and non-editable elements in one go. In a print shop scenario, that means your colleague’s fuzzy logo PNG or a client’s hair-bleed photo can be fixed in seconds – not hours.
                </p>
                <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-0">
                    However, Corel stresses (and we concur) that these are assistive tools, not full replacements. For example, while AI can draft an image from scratch, a human still must ensure brand colors, typography, and output settings are correct. In short, generative AI is “fully integrated” and fun to use, but Corel’s goal is to inspire the artist, not replace them.
                </p>
            </div>

            <h3>Credits & Usage Limits</h3>
            <p>
                All AI generation uses Corel’s AI credit system. Subscribers and maintenance customers get <strong>2,000 credits per month (renewing)</strong> – enough for dozens of medium-sized images. One-time (perpetual) buyers get 2,000 credits once (no renewals). If you run out, credits are available for purchase (Corel’s pricing table shows packs from 2,000 to 35,000 credits). Note that credits don’t roll over between months, so heavy AI users must monitor usage. Overall, the AI tools in 2026 are powerful and well-implemented, but they come with a metered system and require a modern GPU for best performance.
